I'm actually slowly working on a patch based on the previously
mentioned Enhanced Artifacts patch by Nephi. Many of the ideas
presented by the OP are present in this patch (so much so that I
suspect he's at least somewhat familiar with it), and are very good
ideas.

The very best idea, of course, is that all the various banes (ie all
artifacts that target specific creatures) are made more useful by
granting various intrinsics or properties that are generally useful,
and especially useful (or plausibly useful) in fighting the targeted
monster. So granting reflection to Dragonbane and hungerless
regeneration to Trollbane offsets the uselessness of their narrow
scope, as well as helping battle dragons and trolls. Nephi also gives
them targeted warning (a la Sting) and +d20 instead of x2 damage,
decreasing the need for enchanting the artifact or expending skill
slots. (Nephi's patch does a bunch more; go check it out)

I have found it useful to group the artifacts in categories when
thinking about changes and balance issues (rearranged from Dylan
O'Donnell's spoiler):

ELEMENTAL
Fire Brand
Frost Brand
Grayswandir	/* The Pattern is elemental */
Mjollnir

BANES
Sting
Orcrist
Demonbane
Sunsword
Dragonbane
Trollsbane
Ogresmasher
Giantslayer
Werebane

SHARP /* non-magical weapons that do extra physical damage */
Grimtooth
Snickersnee
Cleaver
T Muramasa /* It bisects, but because it is really sharp */

CROWNING GIFTS
Excalibur
Vorpal Blade
Stormbringer

OTHER MAGICAL WEAPONS
L Diana
Magicbane
S Aesculapius
S Might

ETC
Eye Aethiopica
Eyes Overworld
H Ahriman
M Mirr Merlin
Mitre Holiness
M Key Thievery
O Detection
Orb of Fate
PYEC

There are some patterns, of course. The elemental and magical artifact
weapons (save magicbane) all do double damage whereas the mundane
'sharp' weapons do an extra die roll of damage. The crowning gifts
are, in this respect, sharp weapons that also have magical
properties.

If the banes are all made to be more powerful, then if you wanted to
maintain balance you could add mundane 'sharp' artifacts to dilute the
artifact pool. In order to make them more interesting, you could make
these new artifacts underutilized weapon types that may encourage
players to ascend with something else besides a flavor of long sword.
Artifact polearms, knives, spears (Dragonbane could be a spear, btw,
since spears are given +tohit against Dragons in the game), whatever,
as long as little or no magical properties are granted. Actually, the
game itself could probably create random non-magical artifact weapons
and choose the name from a list of compelling names. Hmm...

Finally, I'd like to mention a couple more good ideas that I've ran
across in case the OP is actually going to make a patch. L (http://
www.greyfire.org/~l/nethack.html) has a couple of artifact patches
that grant the Heart of Ahriman displacement ("It seems to be in two
or more places at once, even though you are holding it.") and strength
(like GoP) to the Sceptre of Might ("As you pick up the Sceptre of
Might it seems heavy at first, but as you hold it strength flows into
your arms."). In these cases, the game itself is offering a YANI.

Just my two cents.

